# Author: Daen
# Version: 1.0
# Meridiano — Konsole palette
#
# ── Why it is not monochrome ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The theme's rule is that colour carries meaning, not decoration. In a
# terminal colour *is* meaning: red is an error, green is a success. An
# all-grey palette here would do real damage — a compiler error would
# look like ordinary text.
#
# So the rule is a different one: the shades are all warm and low in
# saturation, and there is exactly one live colour, amber, which is the
# accent everywhere else in the theme too. The cold tints the default
# terminal uses — bright cyan, blue, acid green — are pulled down and
# pulled warm until they look like part of the same instrument rather
# than three arbitrary tints.
#
# ── The numbers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every colour is on the theme's ramp, from the #0e0d0b background to
# the #e8e4dc text, with two exceptions: the amber #ff9d2e, which is
# the accent, and the brick red #d8492f, which is the alarm. They are
# the same two values as the colour scheme's and the telemetry panel's,
# so an error has the same colour across the whole desktop.
#
# Black is not black. On a near-black background pure black is
# invisible, and the edges of blocks disappear: #0e0d0b on #0e0d0b is
# a line that is not there. That is why Color0 is raised to #1e1c19.
[Background]
Color=14,13,11
Opacity=1.0

[BackgroundFaint]
Color=20,20,17
Opacity=1.0

[BackgroundIntense]
Color=26,25,23
Opacity=1.0

[BackgroundFaintIntense]
Color=32,30,27
Opacity=1.0

